<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
        <artifactId>rains-flexible-modules</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>mannequin-standalone</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Rain's Mannequins</name>
    <description>
        Rain's Mannequins, as a plugin of its own. Nothing here but a build file and a descriptor: the
        mannequin module and the standard wrapper are shaded together, and that is the whole plugin.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!-- Terminal deployable plugin jar, not a Maven library: never published to the registry. -->
        <maven.deploy.skip>true</maven.deploy.skip>
        <!-- The name the server sees, and the name of its data folder. -->
        <plugin.name>RainsMannequins</plugin.name>
        <!--
          1.0.0: the first release of this as a plugin. There is no earlier version line.

          1.1.0: the loadout GUI's material picker silently dropped an item chosen without also
          being enchanted, and editing several slots in one visit could overwrite an earlier
          change with a later one built from the same stale snapshot. Added the missing menu
          entirely — a list of a player's own mannequins and an edit hub for one, matching every
          other module's shape — which also fixed the redstone signal never firing at all: nothing
          had ever called the code that places the barrel for a mannequin switched on after
          creation. A mannequin now also eats a dropped golden apple, not only a potion, found on a
          periodic sweep rather than a pickup event a mannequin's lack of AI never triggers. And a
          death no longer wipes the training tally it exists to record.

          1.2.0: the redstone signal never actually reached a comparator — confirmed as
          PaperMC/Paper#505, an optimisation that stops a plain container update from making an
          adjacent comparator recheck it; fixed by force-updating the comparator's own block state
          directly. The enchant screen offered ten hand-picked enchants in a single seven-slot row,
          so anything past the seventh silently overwrote an earlier button instead of ever
          appearing — replaced with every registered enchantment, paginated, read live from the
          server's own registry. Loadout slots now show the real enchanted item as their button,
          not a generic icon that only mentions the enchants in text. Giving a mannequin an offline
          player's skin silently produced the default skin, because nothing ever asked their cached
          profile to actually resolve its textures — fixed with completeFromCache(), with an async
          Mojang lookup as a fallback for a profile the cache genuinely never saw. Added "copy my
          current gear" to the loadout screen, and a per-mannequin damage leaderboard (ranked by
          player, weapons one click deeper, each with the real weapon last used and its own combat
          numbers) reachable from the stats screen. Removed the "would have killed an unarmored
          player" hit feedback — it made sense while a mannequin could not die at all; now that it
          can, the dummy dying already says that, and a dummy with more health surviving a
          "lethal" hit had nothing on screen explaining why.

          1.3.0: added a "type a username" option to the skin screen — the player chooser can only
          ever offer somebody who has already joined this server, so anybody else (Notch, a friend
          from elsewhere) was simply not reachable; resolved through Paper's own async profile
          lookup, the same one vanilla uses. Corrected a mistaken claim in 1.2.0's own code comment
          that PaperMC/Paper#505 was "confirmed, still open" — it was closed as fixed in 2016, so
          the comparator-refresh workaround from that release stays as harmless defence in depth
          rather than the actual fix it was described as. Added a debug log line to every redstone
          pulse (exact block, item count, tick length) and put the barrel's exact coordinates in
          front of the owner the moment the signal is switched on, since the most likely real
          reason a comparator reads nothing is a build one block too high — the barrel sits a full
          block under the mannequin's feet, not beside them.

          1.4.0: added four more mannequin kinds alongside the original humanoid one — Zombie,
          Skeleton, Wither and Iron Golem, each a real entity of that type with AI/targeting/gravity
          disabled and knockback cancelled, creatable through a new GUI kind-picker screen (reachable
          from the mannequin list, not only /mannequin create) as well as the command with a new
          optional argument. Loadout only applies to the kinds that have real equipment slots
          (player/zombie/skeleton); skin only ever applied to the player kind.

          Also fixed two real, pre-existing bugs found along the way: the shield and redstone
          toggles on the per-mannequin edit page were never actually visible or clickable at all —
          that page was built with three rows, and the row those two buttons live in is the exact
          row Core's own menu framework reserves for the Back/Danger/Close buttons on a three-row
          menu, so every write to it was silently rejected. This was very likely the entire reason
          the redstone signal "didn't work" through every previous release, more than anything about
          comparator-refresh mechanics. And mannequin skins were rendering without their second
          layer (jacket, hat, sleeve and trouser overlays, cape) — nothing had ever told the entity
          to show them.
        -->

        <!--
          1.5.0: non-player kinds now default to that kind's own vanilla-realistic max health
          (a Wither starts at 300, an Iron Golem at 100) rather than a bare player's 20, unless an
          owner sets their own override — which also fixes a Wither's native boss bar reading as a
          strangely low-health Wither. A hostile mob could still attack an Iron Golem (or any other
          non-player kind) even with its own AI disabled, since targeting is decided by the
          attacker, not the target; a tracked mannequin is no longer a valid target for anything.
          Fixed two more deprecated-API warnings found on boot (Wither#setInvulnerabilityTicks,
          the deprecated org.bukkit.event.entity.EntityKnockbackEvent — both replaced with their
          non-deprecated Paper equivalents). A freshly created mannequin now faces the direction its
          owner was facing, instead of always due south. Added a "Rename" option to the mannequin's
          edit page.
        -->

        <!--
          1.5.1: the redstone signal reportedly still not working traced to two real causes on the
          live test server, neither one the barrel/comparator mechanics themselves — the specific
          mannequin under test simply had its own redstone toggle switched off (a fresh mannequin
          does not inherit an earlier one's settings, correctly), and separately, turning on debug
          logging to check that was doing nothing at all: RainsCore's own DEBUG level maps to
          java.util.logging's FINE, which the console's own default threshold silently drops
          regardless of RainsCore's own "console-level: debug" setting — fixed in RainsCore itself
          by logging a debug line at INFO with its own [DEBUG] label instead. Also fixed: a
          mannequin was left setPersistent(true), so a plain server restart produced two of it — the
          one this module spawns fresh from its own stored record on every enable(), standing on top
          of the one the world file itself remembered from before the restart, since vanilla was
          never told not to save and reload this entity on its own. Now non-persistent and tagged
          with its own id, with a boot-time sweep that removes any already-duplicated copy standing
          at its anchor from before this fix existed.
        -->

        <!--
          1.5.2: the redstone signal reaching the barrel's item count exactly as designed (visible
          in the pulse's own debug line, once 1.5.1 fixed that line from being invisible) still left
          every comparator reading 0, confirmed by hand: filling the same barrel through a plain
          vanilla command instead of this module worked immediately, filling it through the pulse
          itself did not. Root cause: the pulse filled the barrel's inventory through
          Block#getState()'s detached snapshot copy, then wrote it back with update() — which changes
          what the barrel holds but skips whatever a live container mutation (a player's own click, a
          hopper, that same vanilla command) does to tell an adjacent comparator to recompute. Fixed
          by operating on Block#getState(false), the real block entity Paper itself documents as "to
          operate on real block data" — the same live path every other container interaction takes.
        -->
        <plugin.version>1.6.0</plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>mannequin-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>modules-wrapper</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <finalName>${plugin.name}-${plugin.version}</finalName>
        <resources>
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
                <!-- The descriptor names the version, and a version written twice is one that drifts. -->
                <filtering>true</filtering>
            </resource>
        </resources>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
              StandaloneJarTest reads the jar this build produces, so it has to run after it exists.
              Surefire bound to a later phase rather than failsafe, which is one more plugin to have
              cached — this build has to work offline.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>default-test</id>
                        <phase>none</phase>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>after-the-jar-exists</id>
                        <phase>integration-test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>test</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <skipTests>false</skipTests>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
